ll sometimes refer to as the  orbital angular momentum  when we want to emphasize that we are  only discussing orbital angular momentum  we will typically label angular momentum  with the letter  l as we've been doing so far  and similarly for the corresponding  computation relations  but we also have this new type of  angular momentum  that only exists in quantum mechanics  that we call  the spin angular momentum when we want  to emphasize that we're discussing  spin angular momentum we will typically  label  angular momentum with the letter s  similarly for the communication  relations  so you will see angular momentum labeled  by all of l  s or j l and s are used when we want to  clarify  orbital versus spin angular momenta  while j  simply refers to a general angular  momentum which could be  either or a combination of both orbital  and spin angular momenta  as an example of using the general  angular momentum j  and understanding the consequences of  the defining computation relations  let's consider the angular momentum  magnitude  as j is a vector operator with three  components  we can define a magnitude for it as j  squared  which is j dot j in terms of components  this becomes j 1 squared plus j 2  squared  plus j 3 squared this operator is  trivially her mission  because each component ji is hermitian  and in turn this means that j squared is  an observable and we will see in other  videos that it plays a  major role in the quantum theory of  angular momentum  but today let's simply calculate its  commutative with j1  we get these three commutators by using  the definition of j squared  the first one trivially vanishes because  j1 commutes with itself  and for the other two we can expand it  in the usual way  to obtain these four terms  using the defining commutation relations  for angular momentum  these two commutators are equal to minus  ih bar j3 and these two commutators  are i h bar j two  we can see that this term cancels with  this term  and there's one with this one so that  the commutator vanishes  we can repeat the same exercise for the  other components  and they will vanish  and that's it we're done we have defined  angular momentum in quantum mechanics as  any set of three observables that obey  these commutation relations  the fact that the components of the  angular momentum operator in quantum  mechanics don't commute  is a rather remarkable result you will  remember from the video on compatible  observables  that if two observables o1 and o2 don't  commute  then we cannot find a common set of  eigenstates  you will probably also remember from the  video some measurements that if two  observables don't commute  then when we measure one of them we  modify the probability of  finding a given outcome when we measure  the other  we sometimes describe this feature as  saying that we cannot measure  two non-commuting observables  simultaneously  and you will also remember from the  video on the heisenberg uncertainty  principle  that if two observables don't commute  then they are subject  to an uncertainty principle as an  example  remember the videos on the position and  momentum operators which don't commute  with each other  and this means that quantum systems  behave in dramatically different ways  compared to classical systems  for example representing a quantum state  in the position of momentum  representations  in terms of the corresponding wave  functions if we have a very narrow wave  function in the position representation  then this means that we have a very wide  wave function in the momentum  representation  and the opposite also happens and in  general they are related by a fourier  transform  with angular momentum we are taking this  quantum weirdness  one step further it is in two different  quantities like position and momentum  that don't commute  but instead it is the different  components of the same quantity  angular momentum that don't commute  this makes the theory of angular  momentum in quantum mechanics extremely  rich  intriguingly we have found if you  remember in the previous slide  that j squared does commute with each of  the angular momentum components  you should remember this result because  it'll prove useful as we learn more  about the topic  and we'll just leave it here for now  angular momentum in quantum mechanics is  not restricted to orbital angular  momentum  instead we can define a general angular  momentum  j that includes the orbital angular  momentum  but it can also include other types of  physics  so check out our other videos to find  out more about original angular momentum  spin and mixed angular momentum and as  always  if you liked the video please subscribe

